WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.469
-Welcome back.
We are here with Jerry Seinfeld.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:05.004
His new book,
"Is This Anything?",

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.005
is available now.

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:07.039
Did I do you already?

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:08.908
-No, you just --
You're the only person

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.444
that says it the right way.

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.013
That's the way comedians say it.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:15.381
-We're about to do
something fun.

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:17.516
This is the
"Seinfeld Challenge."

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:20.052
-♪ Seinfeld, Seinfeld,
Seinfeld, Seinfeld,

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:22.322
Challenge ♪

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:24.957
-We asked our viewers
to pick up your book

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:27.860
and record themselves doing
their favorite Seinfeld routine.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.062
Your book has stand-up,
every bit that you've ever done

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:31.264
from the '70s until today.

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:32.598
There's a lot to choose from.

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.733
Tonight, we're gonna watch some
of these submissions

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:35.735
and see how they did.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:38.671
First up, we have
Ashley Gutermuth

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.773
from Wrightstown, New Jersey.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:45.010
She's doing your bit about
talk-show hosts.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.346
Let's take a look at this.

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:49.448
-How come talk-show hosts
never have any idea

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:50.950
how much time they have?

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.818
They're always looking
off-camera.

00:00:52.818 --> 00:00:54.853
"Do we -- do we have time
for this?

00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:56.455
How we doing on time?

00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:57.990
Are we out of time?

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.425
We're out of time."

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:02.461
It's your own show.

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:06.098
Why do you know nothing
about how it works?

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:09.001
You never see
Magnum, P.I., going,

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:11.771
"Are we gonna -- we gonna
strangle this guy?

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.173
or are we gonna take a break?

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:15.508
I tell you what.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.510
I'll bonk him on the head.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.046
We'll take a quick break,
come back.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.148
I'll drive in the car
real fast, okay?

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:23.416
Stay with us."

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.151
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.220
-That was great.
-That was great, right?

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.355
-That was really good.
-That's funny.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.557
-She seemed to have it
memorized, too.

00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:34.527
She got every word right.
-And crushed it. Yeah.

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:37.296
-Do you know that bit?
-Yes. I love that bit.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:39.598
Was that -- Did you do that
on "Letterman"?

00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:41.401
-Many years ago,
yeah, in the '80s.

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:45.171
"Magnum, P.I." gives it away.
-Yep.

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:49.475
Alright, next up,
we have Hans Hoefer

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.977
from Issaquah, Washington.

00:01:51.977 --> 00:01:55.014
This is a classic bit.
This is the McDonald's bit.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.016
-Oh. Uh-huh.
-Alright. Let's see.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.686
-The McDonald's sign --
what is that up to now

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:03.222
Like 89 billion hamburgers?

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:04.657
89 billion.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.226
What an outrageous number.

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:09.228
I mean, I would really like
to sit down with

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:13.333
the C.E.O. of McDonald's
and say, "Look, we get it.

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.868
You've sold a lot of hamburgers.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:20.373
89 billion, quadrillion,
cotillion hamburgers,

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:21.974
whatever that is."

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:24.510
Why not just put up
a sign that says,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.680
"McDonald's --
we're doing very well."

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.017
-I think he landed.
I think he landed.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:35.121
-The fun part of that bit is --
I want to bump into the --

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.590
I would love to meet the
chairman of McDonald's

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.526
so I could go,
"Look, we all get it."

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.829
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:44.263
-Yeah, you're right.
-That's the fun part.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:45.898
-That's the fun part, yeah.
-That's the attitude.

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:48.201
-He had added props
and he had kids' toys,

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:51.103
so I'll give him points
for that one.

00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:55.308
But this one --
Here's Magda Mihaila from France

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:58.778
trying your routine "Mad Cow."

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:01.814
-What I love about the
mad cow disease is that,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:05.485
of course,
we try to blame it on the cows.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:09.689
"It's not our fault.
These cows are crazy."

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:12.659
They're nuts.
They're out of their minds."

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.461
We show them sliding down
the slippery ramps,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.929
all out of control.

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:19.631
And the cows
are probably like,

00:03:19.631 --> 00:03:22.868
"Oh, sure, you're drinking me,
eating me, wearing me,

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.971
sneaking up on me,
and tipping me over,

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:27.607
and I'm the one who's off?"

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:30.877
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.347
-In front of cows.
-In front of cows.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:36.181
That's the next level
of comedy is,

00:03:36.181 --> 00:03:40.186
you actually go to the premise.

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:41.353
-To the joke.

00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:42.621
-Yeah, of the joke.
Yeah, you're there.

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:44.324
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:46.959
I have one more
here to do -- Phew!

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.095
Here to do your bit about
the post office.

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:52.865
-Oh.
-Watch me. Watch this.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:55.201
[ Clears throat ]
-Oh. Oh!

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:56.636
-Yeah. Here we go.

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:58.671
-Okay.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:04.744
-The post office always has this
emotional, financial meltdown

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:08.981
every 3 to 5 years that
their business model from 1630

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:10.716
isn't working anymore.

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:15.521
I can't understand how a
21st century information system

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:19.992
based on licking, walking,
and a random number of pennies

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.660
is struggling to compete.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.695
What is the reason?

00:04:23.695 --> 00:04:26.231
Makes no sense.

00:04:26.231 --> 00:04:29.368
Then they always push the
Postmaster General

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.137
out on TV to explain
their differences.

00:04:32.137 --> 00:04:33.472
Always have to make
a big speech.

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:34.940
He's all freaked out.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.644
He has rings under his eyes,
no shave, pulling all-nighters.

00:04:38.644 --> 00:04:40.513
"We can't do it anymore!

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:43.649
We got to go up a penny
on the stamps!

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:45.918
There's no way around it!

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.220
We're trying to get some
breathing room!

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.589
The costs, the infrastructure!"

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:55.294
And we're all like,
"Dude, dude, relax.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:56.595
Raise it a penny.

00:04:56.595 --> 00:04:58.965
We don't even know how much
a stamp is anyway.

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:02.267
44, 53, 62.

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:03.636
Make it a buck.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.072
You're gonna get there.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.708
And if it ends up you have
some money left over,

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:12.144
buy yourself some pants
and a real car."

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:14.213
♪♪

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.648
-Yes!

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.250
-Alright.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.785
Do you remember that bit?
-That was excellent.

00:05:19.785 --> 00:05:23.188
You brought the maturity
of the years,

00:05:23.188 --> 00:05:28.327
the greater -- the condescension
that only comes with age.

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:32.164
-The new Jerry impression,
you go,

00:05:32.164 --> 00:05:35.634
"What is the --"
You sound like that almost.

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:40.273
"The guy's going in-to-the-thing
with the post...

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:42.408
No, the thing I don't get
about that..."

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:43.576
And you do that.

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:46.012
Then you sprinkle in a, "No!"

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:48.114
If you want. If you want to.

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:50.049
That's when you're already
riding the wave.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:51.384
That's showing off.

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.585
That's when you hang ten and
you do something --

00:05:53.585 --> 00:05:54.887
you show off a little bit.

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.323
And "seen" is a good word
to do you, by the way.

00:05:57.323 --> 00:05:59.124
One-word impression.

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:01.860
"Seen."
-Seen?

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.963
-Yeah, like,
"Have you seen yourself?"

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:05.097
-Oh, right.

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:07.633
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.034
"Have you seen..."

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:13.138
"Seen" is the one-word
impression for the new Jerry.

00:06:13.138 --> 00:06:16.175
-"Have you seen yourself?"
is a hilarious line

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:20.879
that I do not have anywhere,
but it's a perfect line for me.

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:22.682
"Have you seen yourself?"

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:25.284
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:27.587
That's it.
I should write for you.

00:06:27.587 --> 00:06:28.887
-Yeah.

00:06:28.887 --> 00:06:32.525
-"Right. I understand.
But have you seen yourself?"

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:35.127
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:37.229
"Have you seen yourself?"
is the next bit.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:38.564
I want to see it, please.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:41.667
Alright, Jerry, unfortunate --
it's a tight race,

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.735
but there can only be
one winner.

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:46.872
-Ashley Gutermuth is my winner.

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:48.173
-I agree. There you go.
♪♪

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:51.110
-She memorized the bit.
-Ashley.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.845
I totally agree, yep.
-She performed it very well.

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:55.748
It's not even that funny,
but she made it funny.

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:58.550
-We're gonna send her an
autographed copy of your book...

00:06:58.550 --> 00:06:59.685
-Oh, fantastic.

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:01.521
-...and a little
"Tonight Show" swag.

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:03.155
My thanks to everyone
who participated in

00:07:03.155 --> 00:07:04.356
the "Seinfeld Challenge."

00:07:04.356 --> 00:07:06.658
And, of course, Jerry Seinfeld,

00:07:06.658 --> 00:07:08.294
thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:11.296
It's always the best time.
I love you, buddy, and be safe.

00:07:11.296 --> 00:07:12.765
-So good to see you.
Love you, too.

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:14.200
Happy Thanksgiving, everybody.

